<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nyangwe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nyangwe is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba River</a> in Maniema in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. In the nineteenth century it was a major market of the Swahili-Arab trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> witnessed a massacre in 1871. Its capture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> forces in 1893 was a decisive moment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-arab-war">Congo Arab war</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nyangwe.jpg" alt="Nyangwe" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Livingstone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nyangwe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
